# examples/spec/fpaxos.cml: Flexible Paxos as an abstract model,
# extending the shared consensus base.  It inherits Agreement (at most
# one value chosen) and demonstrates Heidi Howard's central result.
#
# Reference: Heidi Howard, Dahlia Malkhi, Alexander Spiegelman,
#   "Flexible Paxos: Quorum Intersection Revisited", OPODIS 2016,
#   arXiv:1608.06696.
#
# Classical Paxos requires every quorum to be a majority so any two
# quorums intersect.  FPaxos shows the only intersection the safety
# argument uses is between a Phase 1 (leader-election) quorum and the
# Phase 2 (replication) quorums of earlier leaders.  So the real
# requirement is that every Phase 1 quorum (size Q1) intersect every
# Phase 2 quorum (size Q2); a sufficient instance is Q1 + Q2 > A.
# Majority is the special case Q1 = Q2 = floor(A/2)+1.
#
# The model: each acceptor accepts one value (Phase 2); a value is
# chosen once a Phase 2 quorum (Q2 acceptors) holds it.  A new leader
# proposes value v only after a Phase 1 read of Q1 acceptors that saw no
# other value -- which is possible exactly when some Q1 acceptors avoid
# the holders of that other value, i.e. A - (holders of w) >= Q1.  When
# Q1 + Q2 > A this cannot happen once w is chosen (A - Q2 < Q1), so the
# new leader always sees the chosen value and cannot propose a different
# one -- Agreement.  The config here is A = 5, Q1 = 4, Q2 = 2, so
# Q1 + Q2 = 6 > 5: safe, with a cheap 2-acceptor steady state.
#
# -DBUG: accept_bug uses the Phase 2 quorum size (Q2) where the Phase 1
# guard needs Q1 -- the classic FPaxos mistake of shrinking the
# replication quorum without enlarging the election quorum.  With
# A - (holders of w) >= Q2 the new leader's read can miss a chosen
# value, so two values are chosen.
#
#   cmc --spec examples/spec/fpaxos.cml
#   cmc --spec examples/spec/fpaxos.cml --define BUG   (Q1 shrunk to Q2)

module fpaxos
extends consensus

const A = 5                     # acceptors 0 .. A-1
const Q1 = 4                    # Phase 1 (leader-election) quorum
const Q2 = 2                    # Phase 2 (replication) quorum

# accepted[a] = the value acceptor a has accepted (0 = none).  Each
# acceptor accepts one value in this single-era model.
var accepted : [0 .. A-1 -> 0 .. V]

init accepted == [a in 0 .. A-1 -> 0]

# acceptor a accepts value v (Phase 2).  It may do so only if, for every
# OTHER value w, either w is not yet chosen (fewer than Q2 hold it) or a
# Phase 1 read of Q1 acceptors could have missed w (A minus w's holders
# is at least Q1, so Q1 acceptors avoid every w-holder) -- i.e. the
# leader proposing v legitimately did not see w.  When Q1 + Q2 > A this
# is false once w is chosen, so a different value cannot be accepted.
action accept(a in 0 .. A-1, v in 1 .. V):
  when accepted[a] == 0 and (all w in 1 .. V : w == v or (count c in 0 .. A-1 : accepted[c] == w) < Q2 or A - (count c in 0 .. A-1 : accepted[c] == w) >= Q1)
  accepted' = [accepted except [a] = v]

# the planted bug: the Phase 1 guard uses Q2 where it must use Q1 (the
# election quorum was not enlarged when the replication quorum shrank),
# so Q1 + Q2 > A no longer protects it and two values are chosen.
action accept_bug(a in 0 .. A-1, v in 1 .. V) [BUG]:
  when accepted[a] == 0 and (all w in 1 .. V : w == v or (count c in 0 .. A-1 : accepted[c] == w) < Q2 or A - (count c in 0 .. A-1 : accepted[c] == w) >= Q2)
  accepted' = [accepted except [a] = v]

# a value v is chosen once a Phase 2 quorum of acceptors holds it.
action choose(v in 1 .. V):
  when (count a in 0 .. A-1 : accepted[a] == v) >= Q2
  chosen' = [chosen except [v] = 1]
